    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain the mechanism of leucine in triggering the mTOR pathway? also i need to know how a caloric surplus specifically impacts nitrogen balance for someone trying to maximize hypertrophy. thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Leucine is a key initiator of the mammalian target of rapamycin (mTOR) pathway by directly activating mTOR complex 2 (mTORC2) and indirectly stimulating mTORC1. Leucine binding to its receptor (INSL6/STAT3, and IL-9r/STAT5 andSTAT3 has been shown) triggers signaling cascades that stimulate the activation of down-stream effectors and promote protein synthesis and resistance to insulin/IGF-1-induced muscle breakdown. This leads to enhanced cell growth, protein synthesis, and muscle protein repair.\n\nIn terms of nitrogen balance, a caloric surplus will lead to an increased anabolic flux, which will drive a positive nitrogen balance. The excess amino acids from the surplus calories are utilized to synthesize muscle proteins, providing the necessary nitrogen for muscle protein synthesis. A balance between sufficient protein intake and a caloric surplus is necessary for maximizing hypertrophy, and a daily nitrogen balance > 15-20 will be optimal for muscle protein synthesis, building strength and muscle mass.",
      "source": "target",
