      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so if i'm looking to optimize this, i probably need to time my leucine intake around my training windows. how much of a surplus are we talking for a moderate trainee? like is 300-500 kcal extra enough to keep that nitrogen balance positive without just putting on too much fat?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Timing leucine intake around training windows can help optimize muscle protein synthesis. \n\nFor a moderate trainee, a daily caloric surplus of 250-500 calories can be sufficient to maintain a positive nitrogen balance while minimizing the risk of excessive fat gain. This translates to an increase in daily energy intake of 10-25% above maintenance levels.\n\nHowever, it's essential to note that the traditional caloric surplus range of 1-2 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight (e.g., 150-200 grams) is more critical for maintaining and building muscle mass. This range can help ensure adequate protein synthesis and nitrogen balance while minimizing the risk of fat gain.\n\nKeep in mind that individual factors, such as genetics, training experience, and body composition, can influence the required caloric and protein intake for muscle growth.",
